WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
A good font at a good size
can still be tiring to read.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.000
The usual culprits are
lines that run too long,

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.660
and line height that does not
match the size of the text.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.700
You will cap running text with the ch unit,
tighten line height as type gets larger,

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.940
and attach headings to the
content they introduce.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.520
The measure is the length of a line.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.720
For running text, 45 to 75
characters per line is comfortable,

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.600
with about 65 as a sweet spot.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.680
Shorter lines feel choppy.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.140
Longer lines make it hard to
find the start of the next one.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.820
CSS has a unit made for this.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.160
ch is the width of the zero
character in the current font,

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:48.280
so 65ch tracks the typeface
rather than a pixel guess.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:55.560
Before, a help article sat in a full-width
container on a 1440 pixel screen.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.820
Lines ran to about 180 characters
and readers lost their place.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.800
After, the article is
constrained to 65ch and centred,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.400
with the sidebar using
the remaining space.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:14.020
Same content, noticeably easier to read,
and the page looks more considered.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.100
Small text needs generous line height.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:23.180
Large text needs less. A single line-height
of 1.5 on everything makes headings

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.060
look loose and disconnected.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:30.580
Use unitless values so line height
scales with the element’s own font size,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.320
instead of inheriting a fixed
pixel value from a parent.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.940
Vertical rhythm is the
spacing between paragraphs,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.940
headings, and lists. Two
rules cover most cases.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.060
Put more space above a
heading than below it,

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.100
so the heading attaches to
the content it introduces.

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:53.020
Keep paragraph spacing roughly
equal to one line of body text.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:57.380
The star plus star selector adds
space only between siblings,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.800
so there is no stray margin
at the top of the container.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.300
Interface text is different.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.320
Labels, buttons, and table
cells are single lines,

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.340
so measure rarely applies.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.940
What matters there is a consistent
line height so text and icons align.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.700
A common approach is a
fixed line height per step.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:26.140
For example, 16 pixel text on a 24 pixel
line, which also lands on an 8 point grid.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.110
Cap running text at about 65ch.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:34.980
Tighten line height as text gets
larger, and use unitless values.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.740
Space above headings should
be larger than space below.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.360
Next, once the scale and
the measure are set,

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.900
most of the remaining hierarchy
comes from weight and colour,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.840
not from another font size.
